+- [S6] 1. Extract explicit triangular mesh from NeRF. 2. Enable intuitive user-driven deformations through classic ARAP deformation method. 3. Generate tetrahedral mesh around triangular mesh to propagate deformations spatially. 4. Employ tetrahedral vertex interpolation to transform discrete deformations into continuous field. 5. Ensure rays passing through mesh bend according to user adjustments.
+
+#### NeuralEditor
+- [S6] A method employing a point cloud-guided NeRF model for shape manipulation, leveraging K-D trees and deterministic integration strategy for accurate point cloud-based rendering, with Phong reflection modeling to enhance color fidelity and geometric detail.
+- [S6] 1. Employ point cloud-guided NeRF model. 2. Leverage K-D trees. 3. Use deterministic integration strategy. 4. Achieve accurate point cloud-based rendering. 5. Apply Phong reflection modeling.
+
+#### Shared G-MLP
+- [S7] A shared geometry MLP that enables the constraint of multi-view consistency to be propagated from the NeRF branch to the detection branch.
+- [S7] Without the shared G-MLP, performance drops from 50.1 to 48.1 mAP@.25, as the influence of multi-view consistency is only propagated into the image backbone.
 
 ## Background
-The standard NeRF architecture, introduced by Mildenhall et al. [1], uses hierarchical rendering with two networks (coarse and fine) to improve computational efficiency [S1]. Subsequent works have focused on accelerating training and inference, leading to various architectural modifications such as voxel grids [S1], SDFs [S1], and transformer integrations [S1]. Generative approaches like NeRF-VAE [S1] and dynamic extensions like NeRF with time parameters [S5] have also expanded the architectural scope. Editing methods like NeRF-Editing [S6] and NeuralEditor [S6] introduce mesh or point cloud guidance for deformation.
+The original NeRF architecture by Mildenhall et al. [1] employed a hierarchical rendering approach with two networks (coarse and fine) to improve computational efficiency. Subsequent works have focused on accelerating training and inference, with many efforts falling into the categories of architectural modifications, optimization procedures, and representation substitutions. The standard implementation uses dense layers with ReLU activations, with default parameters including a network depth of 8 layers and 256 channels per layer [S3, unofficial].
 
